> > > > > > > (N=146786)
> > > > > > > which is grouped by post code zones (J=1784). There are two
> > > > types
> > > > > > > of
> > > > > > > explanatory variables in the data set: the first at the
> > > > > > > property
> > > > > > level
> > > > > > > (lower-level) and the second at the post code level (upper-
> > > > level).
> > > > > > The
> > > > > > > response is the log value of each house.
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > I am exploring the BYM2 model to account for the spatial
> > > > structure
> > > > > > as
> > > > > > > a random intercept at the upper-level in a multilevel model.
> > > > > > > The
> > > > > > > spatial structure is defined using the 6 k-nearest neighbours
> > > > (of
> > > > > > the
> > > > > > > post codes) represented in a symmetric binary weight matrix
> > > > > > > (W).
> > > > > > > The
> > > > > > > lower-level observations are indexed by an integer to the
> > > > > > > respective
> > > > > > > row-position in the weight matrix W (code attached).
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > I fitted two models, one including all lower-level and upper-
> > > > level
> > > > > > > covariates (inla_mres) and a second ignoring the explanatory
> > > > > > variables
> > > > > > > at the upper-level (inla_mres2). In the results, the precision
> > > > of
> > > > > > the
> > > > > > > random effects at the upper-level decreased from 50 to 20
> > > > > > > (which
> > > > > > > is
> > > > > > > somehow in line with the expectation). However, the mixing
> > > > > > > parameter
