I added some comments in the code for the first model. I think the
agressive number of covariates and data make this fit a little more
challenging, it just behaves differently (I havn't checked really why).

anyway, seems like different initial values are needed for the bym2, and
this gives now a stable fit. this you can verify from the log, like

Compute corrected stdev for theta[0]: negative 0.959  positive 1.045
Compute corrected stdev for theta[1]: negative 0.959  positive 1.043
Compute corrected stdev for theta[2]: negative 0.850  positive 0.693

when the corrections are just around 1. this is a good sign (not always
bad if it isn't but...)

